<html xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word"><head><meta charset=utf-8><title>Karen Lochhead - Full Pack Part 1</title></head><body><h1>Karen Lochhead - Full Pack Part 1</h1><p>Karen Lochhead — Full Notes &amp; Transcripts (Part 1)</p><p>Compiled from Plaud. Includes meeting notes and full transcripts. Being looked at and reviewed.</p><p>03-05 Performance Review</p><p>Plaud file id: 4be028c0842957a15cd7abfaf060c742</p><p>Notes</p><p>### Summary</p><p># Performance Review and Strategic Planning for Karen Lochhead</p><p>This document outlines a performance review meeting between Bob Duncan (manager) and Karen Lochhead (advisor), with Megan Gordon (admin support) also participating. The discussion covers a detailed analysis of Karen&#x27;s recent and year-to-date sales performance, lead conversion rates, and pipeline health, before transitioning to future strategies, income projections, and process improvements aimed at hitting new targets.</p><p>------------</p><p>## Monthly Lead Performance Review</p><p>Bob Duncan initiated the review by examining lead and opportunity data from the previous month. Despite technical delays with the agenda loading, Bob highlighted that lead volume is trending positively, with 28 leads recorded for the last month. Karen Lochhead attributed this increase to the natural seasonal shift coming out of December and into the new year, expressing hope for a further spike throughout March.</p><p>The discussion then drilled down into the specific status of these leads. Bob queried the current categorisation of leads, specifically regarding those marked as &quot;dipped&quot; versus those where clients had opted out. Karen expressed concern that only four clients were at the tipping stage, noting she hadn&#x27;t been actively contacting people recently. Bob pressed for clarity on the seven leads currently marked as &quot;appointment booked,&quot; asking whether these appointments had actually taken place, if the clients had been dipped, or if they were &quot;no-shows.&quot;</p><p>Further analysis of the lead breakdown revealed a mix of stages: four leads were still at the &quot;created&quot; stage, some were marked as &quot;submit appointment completed,&quot; and others as &quot;contacted, keeping in touch.&quot; There was also a segment of &quot;Not Proceeding With&quot; (NPW) leads, including those not interested or unable to contact. Bob emphasised the need to review these classifications to ensure leads weren&#x27;t languishing in the wrong status and to confirm if any &quot;unable to contact&quot; clients should actually be moved to a nurturing stage.</p><p>------------</p><p>## Pipeline Health and Case Management</p><p>The conversation moved to a granular review of Karen&#x27;s broader sales pipeline. Bob identified 13 clients currently sitting in a &quot;dipped&quot; status and challenged Karen and Megan to determine the last point of contact for each to ensure they haven&#x27;t been neglected. Similarly, there are 32 leads across &quot;created&quot; and &quot;appointment booked&quot; statuses—some dating back to October 2025—that require immediate review to establish their current viability and next steps.</p><p>Karen provided specific updates on several cases. She noted that Billy Townsend, a family referral with bad credit, needs to be interviewed. Regarding protection cases, there are currently 12 in progress. Specific actions were identified for several clients:</p><p>*   **Byron Stevens:** Has not responded to outreach; Megan is to attempt contact again.</p><p>*   **Michael Cooper:** Underwriting returned a quote of £164 per month for life cover. Karen has requested he obtain his GP report from Vitality to explore other options, as she hadn&#x27;t originally ticked the box to see it herself. Megan is to book him in to discuss moving forward.</p><p>*   **Lucy Muir:** Described as difficult, she initially declined cover but later claimed she hadn&#x27;t. Karen has sent a detailed email clarifying the situation, and Megan is to follow up based on that correspondence.</p><p>*   **Alexandria P:** Currently working and has medicals pending. Karen suggested Megan reach out to determine her current income and interest in proceeding.</p><p>Bob confirmed that Karen’s mortgage pipeline is healthy, with £250,000 in projected business and all current cases at the &quot;offer&quot; stage.</p><p>------------</p><p>## KPI Analysis and Performance Benchmarking</p><p>Bob presented a quantitative analysis of Karen&#x27;s Key Performance Indicators (KPIs). Year-to-date, Karen has written £145k of business, averaging approximately £14.5k per month. Crucially, her &quot;banked&quot; income ratio stands at 94% of written business, which Bob praised as a strong figure given the typical industry deficit of around 10%. He noted that her income fluctuations mirror the wider team&#x27;s trends, dipping around holidays and Christmas before recovering.</p><p>A key highlight was Karen&#x27;s conversion rate. Despite having received 255 leads, 57% of these (145 leads) were effectively &quot;dead&quot; on arrival (unable to contact or never booked). However, of the clients Karen actually speaks to and supports, she boasts a 61% conversion rate—the highest in the team after Stephen Marlin (67%) and significantly ahead of peers like Paul (55%) and Roland (50%). Bob framed this as a testament to her efficiency: handling fewer leads but extracting more value from them.</p><p>In terms of protection, Karen is performing well with a 45% penetration rate, ahead of the team average of 43.9%. Her average case value and premium are also higher than the team average. Although Q4 performance is currently at 42.9%, Bob encouraged her to aim for a 50% penetration rate by the end of the year, calculating that she needs just five extra protection cases to achieve this. Karen acknowledged health issues as a challenge with her older client base but expressed confidence in tapping into new markets.</p><p>------------</p><p>## Future Outlook, Targets, and Strategic Initiatives</p><p>Looking ahead, Bob projected a positive outlook for the next financial year. Karen has 96 renewal customers lined up (an increase from 95 this year), with a significant spike expected in September. This renewal bank represents approximately £54k in mortgage income and £16k in fees—totalling around £70k of potential income before any new business is written.</p><p>Strategically, the business is implementing a &quot;Platform&quot; system that will automatically move &quot;unable to contact&quot; or stale leads onto a nurture journey. Bob shared data suggesting 35-40% of people on these journeys re-engage, with roughly 30% eventually doing business. This automation is expected to filter out lower-quality leads (the &quot;shite,&quot; as Karen put it), allowing advisors to focus on higher-quality opportunities. Additionally, an initiative to place Karen in the &quot;West End&quot; office for a few days is planned to target the first-time buyer market, which Karen believes will improve her protection penetration as these clients typically have fewer health issues.</p><p>Financially, Karen has £35,000 in projected income sitting in her pipeline right now. Bob urged her to maximise mortgage submissions in the current month and the first half of the next to ensure they pay out within this quarter. He also set a target for her to write £200,000 in business next year, breaking it down to a manageable £16,700 per month—an increase of just £2,000 (or &quot;two crack cases&quot;) from her current average.</p><p>------------</p><p>## Process Optimisation and Team Resourcing</p><p>The discussion concluded by addressing operational bottlenecks. Bob identified immediate report writing as the single most critical improvement for Karen. The current delay in writing reports after giving advice is stalling the process; once the protection side is done, admin can take over. Karen committed to finishing her reports the same day she gives advice, and Megan was instructed to &quot;nag&quot; her to ensure this happens.</p><p>To support the team&#x27;s growth and cover Megan’s upcoming maternity leave (expected mid-June next year), the business has advertised for a &quot;Trainee Mortgage Broker.&quot; This role will initially be admin-heavy for 12 months, mirroring Megan&#x27;s current duties (dipping, applications, underwriting), before transitioning into a broker role. This strategy aims to ensure seamless support for Karen while providing a career path for the new hire. Bob also mentioned upcoming technological improvements, such as &quot;one-click&quot; applications for lenders like Halifax, which currently accounts for 30% of Karen&#x27;s business, further streamlining the admin workload.</p><p>------------</p><p>## Action Items</p><p>**@Karen Lochhead**</p><p>- [ ] Review the 13 customers currently set in &#x27;dipped&#x27; status to determine last contact and current status - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Review 32 leads under &#x27;created&#x27; and &#x27;appointment booked&#x27; statuses to determine next steps - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Review 11 leads under &#x27;appointment completed&#x27; and &#x27;keeping in contact&#x27; (especially from last year) to check status - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Secure 5 extra protection cases between now and the end of the quarter to hit 50% penetration - [End of Quarter]</p><p>- [ ] Write maximum possible mortgage business this month and early next month to ensure payment this quarter - [Mid-March]</p><p>- [ ] Complete suitability reports on the **same day** advice is given - [Immediate/Ongoing]</p><p>- [ ] Ask for referrals consistently on offers and completions - [Immediate/Ongoing]</p><p>- [ ] Ring clients on completions and offers to maintain relationships - [Immediate/Ongoing]</p><p>- [ ] Commit to attending scheduled catch-up meetings and protect that time in the diary - [Immediate/Ongoing]</p><p>- [ ] Be nice to Megan - [Immediate/Ongoing]</p><p>**@Megan Gordon**</p><p>- [ ] Assist Karen in reviewing the 13 &#x27;dipped&#x27; clients and 32 &#x27;created/appointment booked&#x27; leads - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Ensure workflows are in place for the 11 leads in &#x27;appointment completed/keeping in contact&#x27; form this year - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Reach out to **Byron Stevens** again regarding protection - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Book a chat with **Michael Cooper** to discuss his life cover quote and next steps - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Follow up with **Lucy Muir** regarding protection cover, referencing Karen&#x27;s recent email - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] Reach out to **Alexandria P** to check interest in cover and confirm current income - [TBD]</p><p>- [ ] &quot;Nag&quot; Karen (via catch-up emails) to ensure reports are completed same-day - [Immediate/Ongoing]</p><p>### ADVISER WEEKLY PERFORMANCE &amp; ACTION REVIEW</p><p>## Internal Performance Review — Karen Lochhead</p><p>**Content creation date:** 2026-03-05 12:23:31 ---</p><p>## Leads Performance Review (with CRM Discipline)</p><p>### A) Leads — Last Month (calendar month just ended)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **28 leads** received.</p><p>- CRM status mix (as recorded):</p><p>  - **7** = **Appointment Booked**</p><p>  - **4** = **Created**</p><p>  - **13** = **DIP**</p><p>  - **11** = **Appointment Completed / Keeping in Contact**</p><p>  - Remainder in NPW outcomes (**Not Interested / Gone Elsewhere / Unable to Contact**).</p><p>- Year-to-date conversion context (team report referenced):</p><p>  - **255 leads** received YTD (includes uncontactable/no-show).</p><p>  - **50 applications** in pipeline.</p><p>  - **39 completed**.</p><p>  - **~20% conversion** on total leads.</p><p>  - **61% conversion** on **contactable/spoken-to** clients (second highest in team; Stephen Marlin referenced at **67%**).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior weekly/monthly lead totals or stage snapshots provided for comparison.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Lead volume described as **increasing month-on-month** coming out of winter (December → January → February), with expectation of further uplift through March.</p><p>- When clients are contactable/spoken to, **conversion is strong (61%)**.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Appointment outcome discipline gap:** the **7 “Appointment Booked”** leads are not confirmed as attended/no-show/rescheduled/DIP/lost.</p><p>- **Stage hygiene issues:** cases appear to remain in early stages despite progress (e.g., DIP completed but status not moved).</p><p>- **Stale lead risk:** leads sitting in **Created/Appointment Booked** with very old timestamps (examples referenced back to **07/10/2025**), indicating missed progression/closure and/or missing visible workflows/notes.</p><p>- **Ownership clarity gap:** Karen stated she **hasn’t been contacting people consistently**, increasing dependency on Megan’s workflows—this needs to be explicit per case in CRM (owner + next step + due date).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Reporting distortion:** incorrect stages (e.g., no-shows left as “Appointment Booked”) undermine lead-to-appointment/DIP/app MI.</p><p>- **Revenue leakage:** aged leads (including **07/10/2025**) may be silently lost without reactivation or correct closure.</p><p>- **Platform nurture failure risk:** if **product status** isn’t updated when a lead doesn’t proceed (no-show/not going ahead), leads may be routed into the wrong journey or miss nurture entirely.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Validate the **7 “Appointment Booked”** leads:</p><p>  - confirm **attended vs no-show**,</p><p>  - log the outcome (DIP / lost / rescheduled / close),</p><p>  - update CRM status + next step.</p><p>- Audit the **Created + Appointment Booked backlog (32 leads)**:</p><p>  - confirm last contact date,</p><p>  - confirm next action/workflow and owner,</p><p>  - decide **reactivate vs close** (and code correctly).</p><p>- Enforce **product status updates** for any lead that does not proceed (no-show / not going ahead) to support platform nurture journeys.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead (stage decisions, status correctness, appointment outcomes)</p><p>- **Admin/Support:** Megan Gordon (chase/workflow execution where allocated)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant (process discipline risk):** lead flow is improving and conversion is strong when engaged, but **stage hygiene, outcome logging, and ageing leads** are creating drift and unreliable MI.</p><p>---</p><p>### B) Leads — This Month (platform/process updates only)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Lead volumes described as **down**; focus stated as **doing more with fewer leads** (quality/conversion focus).</p><p>- Platform nurture dependency introduced:</p><p>  - **All leads added to platform from 2026-03-02**.</p><p>  - Team-wide: **2,000+ leads** sitting as **“unable to contact”**, planned to move into nurture journeys (requires correct product status coding).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No “this month” lead volume, contact speed, or outcome metrics provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Platform adoption from **2026-03-02** creates a mechanism to reduce long-term wastage in “unable to contact” via nurture journeys.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- CRM/product status hygiene risk explicitly flagged: if a lead is a **no-show** or **not proceeding**, the **product status must be updated correctly**.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Mis-coded statuses will:</p><p>  - keep leads artificially open or miscategorised,</p><p>  - prevent correct nurture journey assignment,</p><p>  - distort adviser MI.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Update product status for any non-proceeding lead (no-show / not going ahead) so platform journeys work correctly.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (expected):** platform nurture should reduce “unable to contact” waste **if** status coding improves.</p><p>---</p><p>## “Where Are My Leads?”</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **DIP pipeline:** **13** clients marked as **DIP**.</p><p>- **Backlog:** **32** leads under **Created + Appointment Booked**, including items referenced back to **07/10/2025**.</p><p>- **Nurture stages:** **11** leads in **Appointment Completed / Keeping in Contact** (some notes referenced in **December and February**; older Aug/Sep items referenced as needing validation).</p><p>- Specific stalled example:</p><p>  - **Billy Townsend** — needs interviewed; **bad credit**; connected family noted (**Jordan and Lauren Townsend**).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior DIP/backlog counts provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Conversion performance is strong **when contact is achieved** (61% on spoken-to/contactable).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Karen explicitly stated reduced chase activity (“I haven’t been contacting people”).</p><p>- Aged Created/Appointment Booked cases show weak recycle/progress/close discipline.</p><p>- Case-level drift present (e.g., **Billy Townsend** still awaiting interview and documented plan).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Structural loss point: a high proportion of leads are **uncontactable/no-show** (context indicated c. **57%** do not convert into a genuine advice conversation).</p><p>- Pipeline inflation: stale/open leads inflate workload and MI, while not generating revenue.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- For **13 DIP** clients: confirm **last spoken to**, next step, and whether progressing to application or moving to nurture/close.</p><p>- For aged leads (including **07/10/2025**): decide **reactivate vs close**, and document outcome.</p><p>- **Billy Townsend:** progress to interview and log next steps, including affordability/credit considerations.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead (progress decisions, interview/strategy)</p><p>- **Admin/Support:** Megan Gordon (workflow/chase where allocated)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Declining (follow-up discipline):** adviser conversion is strong once engaged, but **engagement frequency and pipeline movement** are being constrained by inconsistent chase and stale statuses.</p><p>---</p><p>## Applications in Progress</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Mortgage pipeline described as “sorted”, with multiple cases at **Offer Received / Exchange** stages and exchange dates present.</p><p>- Report referenced **~£250,000 projected business** (leadership view of pipeline value).</p><p>- One specific “Offer Received” case needed checking, but **client name not captured**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior pipeline snapshot provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Late-stage pipeline appears healthy (offer/exchange weighted) with fewer blockers raised than in lead handling.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- **Visibility/SLA gap:** an “Offer Received” case required attention but was not clearly identified in the discussion/MI, creating ownership ambiguity.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Unnamed/unclear actions reduce accountability and increase the chance of missed deadlines or stalled progression.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Identify the specific **Offer Received** case discussed and log in CRM:</p><p>  - current outstanding items,</p><p>  - next contact date,</p><p>  - owner (**Karen vs Megan**).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead (identify case, confirm next step)</p><p>- **Admin/Support:** Megan Gordon (update workflow/CRM if assigned)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** late-stage mortgage pipeline is moving, but a **case identification discipline gap** remains.</p><p>---</p><p>## Protection in Progress</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **12 protection cases** showing “in progress”.</p><p>- Case-by-case actions/issues raised:</p><p>  - **Byron Stevens** — client in Iraq; Karen reached out; **no response**.</p><p>  - **Rachel McKinnon** — “has cover”; needs correct closure/status if complete.</p><p>  - **Michael Cooper** — underwriting outcome; **£164/month** life cover quote; needs client action re: **GP report access from Vitality**. Also flagged as potentially not proceeding due to cost/effort; Karen expects potential handoff to **Rita** once exhausted.</p><p>  - **Lucy Muir** — declined cover dispute; Karen sent detailed email; needs follow-up to confirm proceed/decline and close/update.</p><p>  - **Harriet Adley** — already in Megan’s workflow to phone (day mentioned as Monday; no date captured).</p><p>  - **Alexandria P** — working again; needs re-contact to confirm income and desire to proceed.</p><p>  - **Hussein Alkhal** — noted as sorted by **Mickey**.</p><p>  - **Irving Commandy** — repeatedly appearing; “lots of articles”; unclear next step.</p><p>- Protection performance (penetration/peer comparison):</p><p>  - Karen protection penetration: **45%** (team average **43.9%**; Paul **46.5%**).</p><p>  - Karen average protection case size and average premium: **higher than team average** (exact £ not stated).</p><p>- Target set:</p><p>  - Increase penetration to **50%** requiring **+5 additional protection cases** by quarter end.</p><p>  - Quarter end was referenced as **2026-04-30** in one action set; another section referenced **2026-03-31** as the quarter end requirement.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior protection pipeline count provided; quarter-by-quarter penetration references were provided:</p><p>  - **Q1:** 26%</p><p>  - **Q2:** 43%</p><p>  - **Q3:** 94%</p><p>  - **Q4:** early days (one additional case would move Karen to **42.9%** per discussion example).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Penetration is **ahead of team average** (45% vs 43.9%) with stronger premium/case size.</p><p>- Workflows exist on some cases (Megan confirmed workflows are in place).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Multiple cases stalled due to **lack of client contact** or unclear next step/owner/date.</p><p>- Older/medical complexity cases creating high effort with uncertain outcomes (explicitly raised on **Michael Cooper**).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- **Revenue slippage:** stalled follow-ups after underwriting/email outcomes (e.g., **Michael Cooper**, **Lucy Muir**) risk cases dropping.</p><p>- **MI distortion:** “has cover” (e.g., **Rachel McKinnon**) not closed correctly inflates pipeline.</p><p>- **Quarter-end banking risk:** protection started too late in **April 2026** may be “50/50” to bank before **2026-04-30**.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- **Byron Stevens:** re-contact attempt and log outcome.</p><p>- **Lucy Muir:** follow up post-email, confirm proceed/decline, close/update CRM accordingly.</p><p>- **Alexandria P:** re-contact to confirm employment/income and intent to proceed.</p><p>- **Michael Cooper:** request client obtains GP report from Vitality; progress next step; if no further movement, transfer to **Rita**.</p><p>- **Rachel McKinnon:** confirm “has cover” outcome and close/update protection record if complete.</p><p>- Add **5 additional protection cases** to reach **50%** penetration by the stated quarter-end deadline(s).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Admin/Support:** Megan Gordon (Byron Stevens, Lucy Muir, Alexandria P, Harriet Adley)</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead (Michael Cooper next step + decision to transfer; Rachel McKinnon status confirmation; protection case target delivery)</p><p>- **Other:** Rita (onward handling once Michael Cooper transferred); Mickey (Hussein Alkhal already handled)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Stagnant (case progression):** penetration performance is strong vs peers, but **in-progress case movement** is constrained by contact delays, unclear next steps, and complex underwriting/admin cases.</p><p>---</p><p>## Written Business (YTD / Quarter position only — no “this month” figure provided)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- YTD written (Karen): **145** (value basis referenced as “grand a month”).</p><p>- Average run rate stated: **~£14k per month** (based on YTD total).</p><p>- Last month written: **~£9,000+** (“nine and a bit”).</p><p>- Quarter plan discussed:</p><p>  - Planned quarter total: **£45,000**</p><p>  - Required between **2026-03-05 and 2026-03-31:** **~£40,000** (as stated).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior weekly review written totals provided.</p><p>- Only “last month” figure provided: **~£9,000+** (month name not captured).</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- YTD run-rate described as steady despite seasonal troughs.</p><p>- Competitive momentum noted (Karen “overtaken Paul” after Paul led in Q1/Q2).</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Karen described the previous month performance as “atrocious” (no additional figure beyond ~£9,000+ captured).</p><p>- Historic volatility noted (“eight grand month and a nine grand month” pattern referenced).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Quarter delivery risk: **~£40,000** still required by **2026-03-31**.</p><p>- Lead and protection follow-up discipline threatens conversion of improving lead flow into written outcomes.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Maintain focus on progressing pipeline to deliver **~£40,000** written by **2026-03-31**.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (momentum), with execution risk:** opportunity exists, but delivery is exposed to slippage from CRM/report and follow-up discipline gaps.</p><p>---</p><p>## Banked Business (YTD only — no “this month” figure provided)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- **137 banked** out of **145 written** = **~94%** banked rate.</p><p>- Benchmark referenced: typical **~10% deficit** between written and banked; Karen performing above benchmark.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior banked snapshot provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong conversion from written to banked (94%) indicates limited fall-off.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- “Insurance lag behind” acknowledged; case-level slippage not detailed.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- If protection cases remain stalled (e.g., **Michael Cooper**, **Lucy Muir**), future banked figures may lag even if mortgages complete.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- No additional actions beyond protection progression actions already listed.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser / Admin:** Karen Lochhead / Megan Gordon (per protection actions)</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** banked income described as “starting to ramp back up again” with seasonal recovery, but protection delays remain a forward risk.</p><p>---</p><p>## Renewal / Remortgage Income (Forward Look)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Renewal conversion benchmark (team-wide): **54%–60%** (work-in-progress) vs **MAB average 42%**.</p><p>- Karen renewal volumes referenced:</p><p>  - **This year:** **95 renewals**</p><p>  - **Next year:** **196 renewals** (increase of **101**)</p><p>- Timing insight: spike in **September** implies stronger **December** writing period.</p><p>- Pay-out lag noted: business written in **March** likely pays in **May**.</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No prior snapshot provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Material uplift in forward renewal volume (95 → 196) supports next year’s remortgage pipeline strength.</p><p>- Improvement noted in Karen/Megan working together on streamlining ahead of expected uplift.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Some renewal client disengagement mentioned, but no client list or contact plan captured.</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Timing risk: some output falls into the next financial year due to pay-out lag (March → May).</p><p>- Capacity/process risk: increased future volumes require stronger adviser/admin workflow discipline now.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Streamline adviser/admin process (Karen + Megan) ahead of expected renewal spikes and West End/platform-driven volume.</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead</p><p>- **Admin/Support:** Megan Gordon</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving:** forward renewal volumes are materially higher, but delivery depends on operational discipline (reports, handovers, protected admin time).</p><p>---</p><p>## Projected Income</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Projected income referenced: **~£35,000** (“best part of thirty five thousand”), expected to pay out across **March 2026 and April 2026** (as discussed).</p><p>#### Vs Last Week / Last Month</p><p>- No comparison figures provided.</p><p>#### What’s Working Well</p><p>- Strong projected income base already in place.</p><p>#### What’s Not Working Well</p><p>- Primary constraint is speed of progression/banking (see report completion bottleneck).</p><p>#### Risks / Blockers</p><p>- Quarter-end slippage risk: protection/mortgage cases starting in **April 2026** may be “50/50” to bank before **2026-04-30**.</p><p>- Delayed reports slow admin handover and progression, exposing income timing.</p><p>#### Actions Required</p><p>- Prioritise completion speed:</p><p>  - protection cases to be “on risk” by **2026-03-31** where possible (as stated for best banking odds),</p><p>  - maximise written mortgage business through March/early April (timing guidance discussed).</p><p>#### Action Owner</p><p>- **Adviser:** Karen Lochhead</p><p>#### Trend Insight</p><p>- **Improving (value present), with timing risk:** projected income is strong but exposed to slippage if reports and follow-ups are delayed.</p><p>---</p><p>## CRM Discipline / Case Progress (Reports, bottlenecks, ownership)</p><p>#### Current Position</p><p>- Primary bottleneck identified: **delayed reports after advice** (“leaving the reports”).</p><p>- Process dependency: once protection side is completed, **admin will pick up